<p>The World Health Organization told countries to prepare for more hantavirus cases linked to the MV Hondius after the outbreak reached 11 reported cases, including nine confirmed infections and three deaths. Dr. Xavier Lescure, an infectious disease specialist at Bichat Hospital in Paris, said a French woman who had traveled on the ship was critically ill with the disease’s most severe cardiopulmonary form and was receiving artificial-lung support. WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said confirmed and suspected cases had been reported only among passengers or crew, but warned that the virus’s long incubation period could produce more cases in coming weeks. The MV Hondius, whose passengers and many crew members have been evacuated, was sailing back to the Netherlands for cleaning and disinfection after Spain accepted the ship in the Canary Islands.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GlyUPaPb2ztRPsyTaAm-9Yk4ibUh80swjc76VpdHMLptHlsbHT3oQo05Vqa_Xh7beAXOekVc5TyT6JkX5OABtPWHO-SkITyFqCRoXPoA3JHkxNcf3-StziocJGq8YUq_gv2xYRepGhZ0R7Nl_XGWjDXeaN3bQx5BaPNa-sViIN8kUoVDyTXym81yXra4r5WH5gbYWEIHlEAV2754-rA' alt='The hantavirus-stricken cruise ship MV Hondius is seen at anchor at the port of Granadilla in Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain, Monday, May 11, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Spanish case: The Spanish passenger who tested positive had a slight fever and minor respiratory symptoms but was reported stable, while 13 other evacuated Spanish nationals initially tested negative at the Madrid military hospital.</li><li>French contacts: French authorities said identified contacts were being hospitalized or monitored, with at least 22 contacts reported in France and five French passengers isolated at Bichat Hospital.</li><li>Dutch breach: Radboudumc hospital in Nijmegen quarantined 12 staff members for six weeks after blood and urine from a hantavirus patient were handled without the strictest required procedures.</li><li>Kansas monitoring: Kansas health officials said they were working with the CDC to monitor three people exposed abroad in the Hondius outbreak, while federal officials described public risk as very low.</li></ul><blockquote>There is no sign that we are seeing the start of a larger outbreak - Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: Tedros thanked Spain for accepting the ship, said containment work was not over and urged authorities to keep applying WHO advice as passengers and crew were monitored. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/12/who-head-warns-countries-prepare-more-hantavirus-cases'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>French Health Ministry: Health Minister Stéphanie Rist said authorities had not detected widespread circulation of the virus in France and were applying a precautionary strategy to break possible chains of transmission. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/sante/live/2026/05/13/en-direct-hantavirus-une-ex-passagere-francaise-du-mv-hondius-en-reanimation-a-paris-dans-un-etat-grave_6688609_1651302.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Dutch Health Ministry: Health Minister Sophie Hermans said the situation was different from COVID-19 and that, with current knowledge and measures, Dutch authorities were confident the virus could be kept under control. (<a href='https://minutemirror.com.pk/dutch-hospital-quarantines-12-staff-after-hantavirus-protocol-breach-554544/'>Minute Mirror</a>)</li><li>European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control: The ECDC assessed the risk to the general EU and European Economic Area population as very low and did not recommend broad measures such as travel restrictions. (<a href='https://www.touteleurope.eu/economie-et-social/hantavirus-ce-que-fait-l-union-europeenne-face-a-l-alerte-du-mv-hondius/'>Toute l&#x27;Europe</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/12/who-head-warns-countries-prepare-more-hantavirus-cases'>WHO head tells countries to prepare f